    dict(key='07-three-holders', kind='figure', title='One recording, three people',
         body='three_holders',
         narration="Let's see the core idea on one real example. [[slnc 400]] At the top, the recording of one login: two records, seventy and fifty two bytes. [[slnc 300]] Inside is the password, but the bytes do not show it. [[slnc 600]] Below, four ways to look at it. [[slnc 300]] An observer with no key reads nothing. [[slnc 300]] A developer with the key log reads the login and the answer. [[slnc 500]] Then an attacker steals the server's long term key. [[slnc 300]] With an old static R S A connection, the login opens. [[slnc 300]] With an E C D H E connection, nothing opens. [[slnc 600]] So the key log is a secret file that opens what it lists, and forward secrecy protects old recordings from a stolen server key."),
    dict(key='08-act-1-figure', kind='figure', title='The seventy bytes of one record',
         body='record_bytes',
         narration="First demo: what the wire shows. [[slnc 400]] This is the client's record, seventy bytes, exactly as a recording holds it. [[slnc 500]] Only the first five bytes are readable: the header. [[slnc 300]] One seven, in hex, is type twenty three: application data. [[slnc 300]] Zero three, zero three is version three point three, which every T L S one point three record says. [[slnc 300]] Zero zero, four one is the length: sixty five bytes. [[slnc 600]] Those sixty five bytes are the forty eight bytes of the login, plus one byte for the real content type, hidden inside, plus a sixteen byte tag that detects any change. [[slnc 300]] Five plus sixty five is seventy. [[slnc 600]] The login, the password and even the real type are all encrypted. [[slnc 300]] With no key, nothing opens."),
    dict(key='09-act-1-1', kind='console', title='Act One — What the wire shows',
         body="ONE. What the wire shows.\n  THE CONNECTION (an online store's login, TLS 1.3 style):\n    client sends  POST /login HTTP/1.1 | user=ana&password=hunter2\n    server sends  HTTP/1.1 200 OK | session=7f3a\n  THE CAPTURE (what anyone on the network records):\n    client random     3bd68db307915645... (in the clear)\n    server random     363791a138986206... (in the clear)\n    client -> server  70 bytes, 17 03 03 00 41 ...\n    server -> client  52 bytes, 17 03 03 00 2f ...",
         narration="Here is the real output. [[slnc 400]] The input: the login the client sends, and the server's answer. [[slnc 400]] The capture: the two random values from the hellos, in the clear. [[slnc 300]] Then two records, of seventy and fifty two bytes, both starting with one seven, zero three, zero three."),
    dict(key='10-act-1-2', kind='console', title='Act One — What the wire shows',
         body='ONE. (continued)\n  THE RECORD HEADER, WORKED OUT (client -> server):\n    17       type 23: application data\n    03 03    version 3.3, the same in every TLS 1.3 record\n    00 41    length 65 = 48 text + 1 inner type + 16 tag\n    total    5 header + 65 = 70 bytes\n  THE RESULT (with no key):\n    contains "password"?  false\n    decrypted             []',
         narration='The record header, worked out: application data, version three point three, and a length of sixty five. [[slnc 300]] Forty eight bytes of text, one byte of type, and a sixteen byte tag. [[slnc 400]] The result: the word password is not in the bytes, and nothing decrypts.'),
    dict(key='11-act-2-figure', kind='figure', title='One key log line, part by part',
         body='keylog_line',
         narration="Second demo: writing a key log. [[slnc 400]] At the top, one real line of the file. [[slnc 300]] It has three parts. [[slnc 500]] In amber, the label: client traffic secret zero, the direction from the client to the server. [[slnc 300]] In cyan, the client random: thirty two bytes, the same value the capture shows in the clear. [[slnc 300]] That is how a tool finds the line for a connection. [[slnc 300]] In green, the secret: thirty two bytes that open that one direction. [[slnc 700]] At the bottom, when a client may write the file. [[slnc 300]] With no setting, there is no key log. [[slnc 300]] In development, it writes to the path given. [[slnc 300]] In production, the shop's client refuses to start."),
    dict(key='12-act-2-1', kind='console', title='Act Two — Writing a key log',
         body="TWO. Writing a key log.\n  THE SETTINGS (the client's environment):\n    SSLKEYLOGFILE not set           no key log\n    SSLKEYLOGFILE, APP_ENV=dev      /tmp/keys.log\n    SSLKEYLOGFILE, APP_ENV=production\n      refused: SSLKEYLOGFILE is set in production; refusing to start\n  THE KEY LOG (two lines for this connection):\n    CLIENT_TRAFFIC_SECRET_0 3bd68db30791... 7c6da57caa88...\n    SERVER_TRAFFIC_SECRET_0 3bd68db30791... ea2a4ed1b2fa...",
         narration='The input: three environments. [[slnc 300]] Not set, no key log. [[slnc 300]] Development, a file. [[slnc 300]] Production, the client refuses to start. [[slnc 400]] The result: two lines for this connection, one for each direction.'),
    dict(key='13-act-2-2', kind='console', title='Act Two — Writing a key log',
         body="TWO. (continued)\n  THE LINE, PART BY PART:\n    label          CLIENT_TRAFFIC_SECRET_0\n    client random  3bd68db30791... (32 bytes)\n                   the capture's: 3bd68db30791... same\n    secret         7c6da57caa88... (32 bytes, one direction)",
         narration="The line, part by part. [[slnc 300]] The label, the client random, which is the same as the capture's, and a thirty two byte secret for one direction."),
    dict(key='14-act-3-figure', kind='figure', title='From the logged secret to the text',
         body='secret_to_key',
         narration="Third demo: reading the capture with the key log. [[slnc 400]] At the top, the lookup. [[slnc 300]] The client random from the capture finds the line, and the line gives the secret. [[slnc 600]] In the middle, how the secret becomes a key, worked out. [[slnc 300]] H K D F expand label, with the word key, gives sixteen bytes: the A E S key. [[slnc 300]] With the letters I V, it gives twelve bytes: the initial value. [[slnc 300]] Each record's nonce is that value, X O R the record number. [[slnc 300]] So record zero ends in hex a seven, and record one ends in hex a six. [[slnc 600]] At the bottom, A E S one twenty eight G C M opens the record, and checks the header too. [[slnc 300]] The login and the password appear."),
    dict(key='15-act-3-1', kind='console', title='Act Three — Reading the capture with the key log',
         body="THREE. Reading the capture with the key log.\n  THE LOOKUP (the capture's client random in the log):\n    client random            3bd68db30791...\n    CLIENT_TRAFFIC_SECRET_0  7c6da57caa88... found\n    SERVER_TRAFFIC_SECRET_0  ea2a4ed1b2fa... found",
         narration="The lookup: the capture's client random finds both lines, client and server. [[slnc 300]] So both secrets are in hand."),
    dict(key='16-act-3-2', kind='console', title='Act Three — Reading the capture with the key log',
         body='THREE. (continued)\n  FROM SECRET TO KEY, WORKED OUT (client direction):\n    key    Expand-Label(secret, "key", 16)  cfeb456056d9d85d...\n    iv     Expand-Label(secret, "iv", 12)   6d3d06a138e98f67...\n    nonce  iv XOR record number: record 0 ends db a7\n                                 record 1 ends db a6\n    AES-128-GCM opens the record, the header is checked too\n  THE RESULT (the records, opened):\n    client: POST /login HTTP/1.1 | user=ana&password=hunter2\n    server: HTTP/1.1 200 OK | session=7f3a',
         narration="From secret to key: the key, the I V, and a nonce for each record number. [[slnc 400]] The result: both records open. [[slnc 300]] The login, with the password, and the server's session."),
